-- Lesson 18 step by step (IELTS-18 v0.3.0): five guided steps and one mixed check on the lesson's passage, in the
-- Lesson 6 format (LEARN FIRST worked example, an explanation for every answer). Owner instruction 2026-10-28 00:10
-- (Asia/Bishkek): put every lesson in steps like Lesson 6, starting with the Reading lessons.
-- Named-student pilot with requires_review; nothing is released. Source: docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json.
-- The lesson passage is written once (art) and appended to each step's LEARN FIRST text; stored content equals the source.
do $steps$
declare art constant text := '

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

THE PASSAGE: The second life of the canals

A. In many British cities, narrow waterways run quietly behind old warehouses and under busy roads. For a few generations they were vital routes for industry; for much of the following century, many were left to decay; today, a large part of the network is busy again, though not with the kind of traffic its builders imagined. Their history is often told as a story of rise and fall, but it is better described as a series of reinventions.

B. Before canals, goods were often carried by packhorse or along rough roads that turned to mud in winter, and a broken load of pottery or a delay of several days could ruin a merchant. The first canals of the industrial age were built in the second half of the eighteenth century to move heavy goods such as coal, which was expensive and slow to carry by road. A horse pulling a boat on calm water could move a far heavier load than the same horse pulling a cart along an uneven track. Investors were quick to see the profits, and within a few decades a network of canals linked major industrial towns with each other and with the coast. Towns that were connected to the network often grew rapidly, while those left out could struggle to compete.

C. Building these routes was a considerable engineering challenge. To take canals across hills, engineers used locks, tunnels and aqueducts. Some tunnels were so narrow that there was no room for a towpath, so the horse was led over the hill while boatmen lay on their backs and pushed the boat through with their legs against the walls. Aqueducts, by contrast, carried canals over valleys and rivers, and some became admired landmarks in their own right. Locks raise and lower boats in stages, but each lock takes time to operate, and a long series of them could slow a journey considerably. Many canals were also built narrow in order to save money, a decision that later limited the size of boats that could use them.

D. The arrival of the railways in the nineteenth century changed everything. Trains were faster, could carry more, and could reach places that water could not. Some canal companies were bought by railway companies, which had little interest in keeping a competitor in good condition, and maintenance was reduced. Independent canal companies tried to compete by lowering their charges, but this left less money for repairs, and a poorly maintained canal became even less attractive to customers. By the middle of the twentieth century, commercial traffic had almost disappeared, and a number of canals had been filled in or blocked.

E. Their revival began with volunteers. From the middle of the twentieth century onwards, groups of enthusiasts spent their weekends clearing rubbish and repairing locks, arguing that canals had value for recreation and for wildlife. Their efforts were helped by a growing interest in industrial history and by the realisation that canals ran through the centres of many towns, where green space was scarce. Restoration was slow and expensive, and some routes remain closed today, but many abandoned stretches have been reopened.

F. The canals now serve very different users. Holidaymakers hire boats, walkers and cyclists use the old towpaths, and a growing number of people live permanently on boats. The water and its banks also provide habitats for birds, fish and plants. In some cities, old warehouses beside the water have been turned into flats, offices and restaurants, and the canal has become a selling point rather than a forgotten backyard. Some campaigners argue that canals could carry freight again and so reduce the number of lorries on the roads, but most transport experts are doubtful, since modern supply chains depend on speed and flexibility that narrow canals cannot offer.

G. The story of the canals is a reminder that infrastructure can outlive the purpose for which it was built. Their engineers would hardly recognise the uses to which their work is now put, yet the canals are arguably more valued today than at any time since their commercial decline. Whether a structure is worth keeping, it seems, depends less on its original function than on what later generations are able to imagine for it.';
begin
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r52-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 18 step 1 of 6: Skim first: one line per paragraph','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Skim the passage in about three minutes, then choose what each paragraph is mainly about.','Skim for a map: the first sentence and a few key words in each paragraph are enough. You will come back for the details.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Paragraph A is mainly about ...", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "supporting_node_id": "6820bff3-06df-4ab2-9437-c0da69fa3906", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  why the railways replaced the canals"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  how the canals were built"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  how the canals have been reinvented several times"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  who lives on canal boats today"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Paragraph B is mainly about ...", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "supporting_node_id": "6820bff3-06df-4ab2-9437-c0da69fa3906", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  how volunteers saved the canals"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  the problems of digging tunnels"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  the price of pottery"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  why the first canals were built and how the network grew"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Paragraph C is mainly about ...", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "supporting_node_id": "6820bff3-06df-4ab2-9437-c0da69fa3906", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  life on modern canal boats"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  the decline of canal traffic"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  the first use of steam engines"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  the engineering methods and their drawbacks"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Paragraph D is mainly about ...", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "supporting_node_id": "6820bff3-06df-4ab2-9437-c0da69fa3906", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  how canals were restored"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  how the railways were built"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  how competition from the railways led to decline"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  why canal companies grew richer"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Paragraph E is mainly about ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "supporting_node_id": "6820bff3-06df-4ab2-9437-c0da69fa3906", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  how the canals were first financed"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  how volunteers began the revival"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  how wildlife returned to the water"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  why some canals were filled in"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Paragraph F is mainly about ...", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "492b5389-b4b7-4efc-b08f-e62d17ce46ff", "supporting_node_id": "6820bff3-06df-4ab2-9437-c0da69fa3906", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  why the railways failed"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  how the canals were engineered"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  who uses the canals today, and a debate about freight"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  how much restoration cost"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 6, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 C, how the canals have been reinvented several times: \"it is better described as a series of reinventions\".\n2 D, why the first canals were built and how the network grew: \"to move heavy goods such as coal\" and \"a network of canals linked major industrial towns\". Pottery is one detail.\n3 D, the engineering methods and their drawbacks: locks, tunnels and aqueducts, then slow locks and narrow canals (\"a decision that later limited the size of boats\").\n4 C, how competition from the railways led to decline: \"The arrival of the railways in the nineteenth century changed everything\".\n5 B, how volunteers began the revival: \"Their revival began with volunteers.\"\n6 C, who uses the canals today, and a debate about freight: \"The canals now serve very different users\", then campaigners and experts disagree about freight.\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Identify gist: skim a full passage to map what each paragraph is about before answering questions."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: skim for a map before the questions

In the test you have about twenty minutes for one passage and thirteen questions. Do not read every word first.
Skim for two or three minutes: read the first sentence of each paragraph and notice a few key words. Write a few words in the margin: A intro, B why built, C engineering...
This map makes every later question faster, especially paragraph-matching.

WORKED EXAMPLE (Paragraph G)
First sentence: "The story of the canals is a reminder that infrastructure can outlive the purpose for which it was built."
Map note: G = the lesson of the story (conclusion). Done in ten seconds.

Now skim and choose the map note for paragraphs A to F.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'0d1c15722318fd56ce6586ea4120c21670e4260aeab424d38577fd42246c9330',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r53-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 18 step 2 of 6: Which paragraph contains ...?','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose the paragraph that contains each piece of information. Use your map from step 1.','Use your map to go to the right paragraph fast, then prove it with the exact words. The same topic in another paragraph is a trap.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "an unusual way of moving boats through tunnels", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "Paragraph A"}, {"value": "B", "label": "Paragraph B"}, {"value": "C", "label": "Paragraph C"}, {"value": "D", "label": "Paragraph D"}, {"value": "E", "label": "Paragraph E"}, {"value": "F", "label": "Paragraph F"}, {"value": "G", "label": "Paragraph G"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "the effect of the canal network on the growth of towns", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "Paragraph A"}, {"value": "B", "label": "Paragraph B"}, {"value": "C", "label": "Paragraph C"}, {"value": "D", "label": "Paragraph D"}, {"value": "E", "label": "Paragraph E"}, {"value": "F", "label": "Paragraph F"}, {"value": "G", "label": "Paragraph G"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "an explanation of why some canals fell into poor condition", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "Paragraph A"}, {"value": "B", "label": "Paragraph B"}, {"value": "C", "label": "Paragraph C"}, {"value": "D", "label": "Paragraph D"}, {"value": "E", "label": "Paragraph E"}, {"value": "F", "label": "Paragraph F"}, {"value": "G", "label": "Paragraph G"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "a reference to people who make their homes on the water", "accepted_answers": ["F"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "Paragraph A"}, {"value": "B", "label": "Paragraph B"}, {"value": "C", "label": "Paragraph C"}, {"value": "D", "label": "Paragraph D"}, {"value": "E", "label": "Paragraph E"}, {"value": "F", "label": "Paragraph F"}, {"value": "G", "label": "Paragraph G"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "a general lesson about old structures", "accepted_answers": ["G"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "Paragraph A"}, {"value": "B", "label": "Paragraph B"}, {"value": "C", "label": "Paragraph C"}, {"value": "D", "label": "Paragraph D"}, {"value": "E", "label": "Paragraph E"}, {"value": "F", "label": "Paragraph F"}, {"value": "G", "label": "Paragraph G"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "a reason why canals in town centres were especially valued", "accepted_answers": ["E"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "Paragraph A"}, {"value": "B", "label": "Paragraph B"}, {"value": "C", "label": "Paragraph C"}, {"value": "D", "label": "Paragraph D"}, {"value": "E", "label": "Paragraph E"}, {"value": "F", "label": "Paragraph F"}, {"value": "G", "label": "Paragraph G"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 Paragraph C: boatmen \"pushed the boat through with their legs against the walls\".\n2 Paragraph B: \"Towns that were connected to the network often grew rapidly\".\n3 Paragraph D: railway owners had \"little interest in keeping a competitor in good condition, and maintenance was reduced\".\n4 Paragraph F: \"a growing number of people live permanently on boats\".\n5 Paragraph G: \"infrastructure can outlive the purpose for which it was built\".\n6 Paragraph E: canals \"ran through the centres of many towns, where green space was scarce\".\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Locate specific detail: match information to paragraphs using a skim map and meaning, not shared words."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: leave paragraph-matching until you know the text

Paragraph-matching questions do NOT follow the order of the passage. That makes them slow if you start with them.
So answer the ordered questions first (TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN, completion). By then you know the passage, and your skim map points you to the right paragraph.

WORKED EXAMPLE
''a reason why canal journeys could be slow''
Map: C = engineering and its drawbacks. Proof in C: "each lock takes time to operate".
Trap: D mentions speed, but it is the speed of trains.  -> Paragraph C

Now match six new pieces of information.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'a10099755b34a8c69b5131a64e550e5bc368e7b56060653d7a8f982020d66f4f',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r54-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 18 step 3 of 6: TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN in order','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN. The questions follow the order of the passage.','Ordered questions move through the text with you: when you find one answer, the next is further on. If one takes too long, move on and come back.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Before canals, bad weather could make roads difficult to use.", "accepted_answers": ["TRUE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Coal was cheap to transport by road.", "accepted_answers": ["FALSE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "People who invested in canals expected to make money from them.", "accepted_answers": ["TRUE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Aqueducts cost more to build than tunnels.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Railway companies that bought canals spent a lot on keeping them in good repair.", "accepted_answers": ["FALSE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Most boats on the canals today are owned by holiday companies.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 TRUE. Proof: \"rough roads that turned to mud in winter\".\n2 FALSE. Proof: coal \"was expensive and slow to carry by road\".\n3 TRUE. Proof: \"Investors were quick to see the profits\".\n4 NOT GIVEN. Both are described in paragraph C, but their costs are never compared.\n5 FALSE. Proof: they had \"little interest in keeping a competitor in good condition, and maintenance was reduced\".\n6 NOT GIVEN. Holidaymakers \"hire boats\", but the passage never says who owns most boats.\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "True/False/Not Given reasoning under time, using question order to locate evidence quickly."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: use the order to save time

TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN questions follow the order of the passage. So:
- Question 2 is after Question 1 in the text. Start looking where the last answer was.
- If you cannot find an answer in about a minute, mark it and move on. The next question will show you where the text has moved to.
- NOT GIVEN answers have no proof, so the next question helps you see you have gone past the place.

WORKED EXAMPLE
''The first industrial canals were built mainly to carry passengers.''
Locate: paragraph B, early. Proof: "to move heavy goods such as coal".  -> FALSE
The next question will be later in B or after it.

Now answer six questions in order.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'23d3bdca7ad26c1749843c525babc89126dfd303447cc59ca8932c94541e7936',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r55-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 18 step 4 of 6: Complete the summary: copy the words','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then complete each sentence with NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage. Copy the words exactly.','Predict the kind of word, find the paraphrase in the passage, and copy the word exactly, with the right spelling and plural.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Before the canals, merchants often moved goods by ________ or along roads that became muddy in winter.", "accepted_answers": ["packhorse"], "taxonomy_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "supporting_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Where a tunnel had no towpath, the horse had to walk over the ________ instead.", "accepted_answers": ["hill"], "taxonomy_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "supporting_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Some of the structures that carried canals over valleys came to be seen as ________.", "accepted_answers": ["admired landmarks", "landmarks"], "taxonomy_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "supporting_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "To win customers back from the railways, independent companies reduced their ________.", "accepted_answers": ["charges"], "taxonomy_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "supporting_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "The volunteers argued that the canals were valuable for ________ and for wildlife.", "accepted_answers": ["recreation"], "taxonomy_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "supporting_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Waterside warehouses were converted into homes, workplaces and ________.", "accepted_answers": ["restaurants"], "taxonomy_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "supporting_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "answer_format": "text"}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 packhorse (paragraph B): \"goods were often carried by packhorse or along rough roads\". Moved goods = carried.\n2 hill (paragraph C): \"the horse was led over the hill\". Copy the singular, as in the passage.\n3 admired landmarks (paragraph C): aqueducts \"became admired landmarks in their own right\". Landmarks alone is also accepted.\n4 charges (paragraph D): \"Independent canal companies tried to compete by lowering their charges\". Reduced = lowering.\n5 recreation (paragraph E): \"arguing that canals had value for recreation and for wildlife\".\n6 restaurants (paragraph F): warehouses \"have been turned into flats, offices and restaurants\". Homes = flats; workplaces = offices.\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Summarise and paraphrase: complete sentences that paraphrase the passage, copying words exactly."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: predict, find the paraphrase, copy exactly

1. PREDICT: read the words around the gap. Do you need a noun, a plural, a verb?
2. FIND: the sentence will use DIFFERENT words from the passage. Look for the same meaning.
3. COPY: write the word exactly as it is in the passage. Spelling and plurals count. No more than two words.

WORKED EXAMPLE
''Engineers overcame high ground with locks, tunnels and ________.''
Predict: a plural noun, a kind of structure. Find: "engineers used locks, tunnels and aqueducts". Copy: aqueducts.

Now complete six sentences.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'16801f22e456b9b29aa04c44cb41cd69680c0eea2b100c11bb2899076ce608ef',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r56-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 18 step 5 of 6: Plan your order: an eight-minute mini-set','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then answer the six questions in eight minutes. Do them in the order you plan, not the order printed.','Ordered questions first, paragraph-matching last, and no answer left blank: guess in the final minute.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Which paragraph mentions a decision that later limited the size of boats?", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "Paragraph A"}, {"value": "B", "label": "Paragraph B"}, {"value": "C", "label": "Paragraph C"}, {"value": "D", "label": "Paragraph D"}, {"value": "E", "label": "Paragraph E"}, {"value": "F", "label": "Paragraph F"}, {"value": "G", "label": "Paragraph G"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Which paragraph compares how the canals are valued now with how they were valued in the past?", "accepted_answers": ["G"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "Paragraph A"}, {"value": "B", "label": "Paragraph B"}, {"value": "C", "label": "Paragraph C"}, {"value": "D", "label": "Paragraph D"}, {"value": "E", "label": "Paragraph E"}, {"value": "F", "label": "Paragraph F"}, {"value": "G", "label": "Paragraph G"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "The first canals of the industrial age were built in the eighteenth century.", "accepted_answers": ["TRUE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Restoring the canals was quick and cheap.", "accepted_answers": ["FALSE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "By the middle of the twentieth century, some canals had been ________ or blocked.", "accepted_answers": ["filled in"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "29a67bc9-2c34-42e6-9f5f-a8b592f7bb3b", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Experts doubt that narrow canals can offer the speed and ________ that modern supply chains need.", "accepted_answers": ["flexibility"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "29a67bc9-2c34-42e6-9f5f-a8b592f7bb3b", "answer_format": "text"}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 Paragraph C: canals were \"built narrow in order to save money, a decision that later limited the size of boats\".\n2 Paragraph G: the canals \"are arguably more valued today than at any time since their commercial decline\".\n3 TRUE. Proof: \"built in the second half of the eighteenth century\".\n4 FALSE. Proof: \"Restoration was slow and expensive\".\n5 filled in (paragraph D): \"a number of canals had been filled in or blocked\". Two words, copied exactly.\n6 flexibility (paragraph F): \"modern supply chains depend on speed and flexibility that narrow canals cannot offer\".\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Strategy selection: choose a question order and manage time across mixed question types on one passage."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: plan the order before you start

You have three question types here, printed in a mixed order:
- Questions 3-4: TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN (follow the passage order)
- Questions 5-6: sentence completion (follow the passage order)
- Questions 1-2: which paragraph? (do NOT follow the order)
Best plan: do 3-4 and 5-6 first, moving through the text. Then do 1-2: by now you know where things are. In the last minute, guess anything still blank. A blank answer always scores zero.

WORKED EXAMPLE
A student starts with Question 1 and spends four minutes searching the whole passage. Questions 5 and 6 are left blank.
Better: Questions 3-6 take about five minutes in order; Questions 1-2 then take about two, using the map.

Now plan your order and answer in eight minutes.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'85050af87e9993ace5756479750c33e16a478bce6971c36ac14edc0ad1e75a3e',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r57-v1','0.1.0','Lesson 18 step 6 of 6: Mixed check','reading','independent_check','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then answer six new questions in eight minutes, in the order you choose. Work on your own, without notes, a dictionary or help. Tell your teacher if anything interrupted you.','Use the whole plan: map, ordered questions first, paragraph-matching last, copy words exactly, and leave nothing blank.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Which paragraph contains a reference to the risk of goods being damaged?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "Paragraph A"}, {"value": "B", "label": "Paragraph B"}, {"value": "C", "label": "Paragraph C"}, {"value": "D", "label": "Paragraph D"}, {"value": "E", "label": "Paragraph E"}, {"value": "F", "label": "Paragraph F"}, {"value": "G", "label": "Paragraph G"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Which paragraph contains a common view of the canals'' history that the writer thinks is misleading?", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "Paragraph A"}, {"value": "B", "label": "Paragraph B"}, {"value": "C", "label": "Paragraph C"}, {"value": "D", "label": "Paragraph D"}, {"value": "E", "label": "Paragraph E"}, {"value": "F", "label": "Paragraph F"}, {"value": "G", "label": "Paragraph G"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Locks allowed boats to change height gradually.", "accepted_answers": ["TRUE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Lowering their charges gave independent canal companies more money for repairs.", "accepted_answers": ["FALSE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Canal holidays are more popular than walking holidays.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "bcddba69-28bc-436c-bb54-01a3c5733099", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Today, a canal can be a ________ for the buildings beside it rather than a forgotten backyard.", "accepted_answers": ["selling point"], "taxonomy_node_id": "fc8a9d7e-3fe4-48a4-9325-84b193ee747c", "supporting_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "answer_format": "text"}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS preparation exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-reading-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 Paragraph B: \"a broken load of pottery or a delay of several days could ruin a merchant\".\n2 Paragraph A: \"Their history is often told as a story of rise and fall, but it is better described as a series of reinventions.\"\n3 TRUE. Proof: \"Locks raise and lower boats in stages\". In stages = gradually.\n4 FALSE. Proof: \"this left less money for repairs\".\n5 NOT GIVEN. Holidaymakers and walkers are both mentioned, but they are never compared.\n6 selling point (paragraph F): \"the canal has become a selling point rather than a forgotten backyard\".\nRead each explanation, then find the proof in the passage yourself.", "focus": "Mixed question types on one passage under time: paragraph matching, TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN and completion."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: the whole plan

1. MAP: you already know the passage; glance at your paragraph notes.
2. ORDERED FIRST: TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN and completion move through the text.
3. MATCHING LAST: use the map to go straight to the right paragraph.
4. NOTHING BLANK: guess in the final minute.

WORKED EXAMPLE
Question: ''Which paragraph contains a proposal for a possible future commercial use?''
Map: F = users today and the freight debate. Proof: "canals could carry freight again".  -> Paragraph F

Now answer six new questions on your own.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_READING_07.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'18124280e3a9c4dc1742bc6c1798ed5a5a3e8fe500a2851253671436b7c50132',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
